WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.299
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.299 --> 00:00:10.009
This work is about transferring our gestures
onto a virtual human's face in a way that

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:11.639
is physically correct.

00:00:11.639 --> 00:00:17.010
This means that not only the changes in the
facial geometry are transferred to a digital

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:18.589
character, no, no, no.

00:00:18.589 --> 00:00:19.589
Here's how it works.

00:00:19.589 --> 00:00:25.429
This piece of work uses a really cool digital
representation of our face that contains not

00:00:25.429 --> 00:00:31.000
only geometry, but there is also information
about the bone and flesh and muscle structures

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.000
as well.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.809
This means that it builds on a physically
accurate model, which synthesizes animations

00:00:36.809 --> 00:00:40.579
where this human face is actuated by the appropriate
muscles.

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:45.489
We start out with a surface scan of the user,
which through a registration step, is then

00:00:45.489 --> 00:00:49.250
converted to a set of expressions that we
wish to achieve.

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:54.829
The inverse physics module tries to guess
exactly which muscles are used and how they

00:00:54.829 --> 00:00:57.679
are used to achieve these target expressions.

00:00:57.679 --> 00:01:02.140
The animation step takes the information of
how the desired target expressions evolve

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:07.520
in time, and some physics information, such
as gravity or wind, and the forward physics

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.260
unit computes the final simulation of the
digital character.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:16.450
So while we're talking about the effects of
gravity and wind, here you can see how this

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:21.900
can create more convincing outputs because
these characters really become a part of their

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.960
digital environment.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:31.960
As a result, the body mass index of a character
can also be changed in both directions, slimming

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:35.820
or fattening the face.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.580
Lip enhancement is also a possibility.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:43.790
If we had super high resolution facial scans,
maybe a followup work could simulate the effects

00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:45.460
of botox injections.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:46.900
How cool would that be?

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:51.590
Also, one of my favorite features of this
technique is that it also enables artistic

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:52.590
editing.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:58.310
By means of drawing, we can also specify a
map of stiffness and mass distributions, and

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:04.500
if we feel cruel enough, we can create a barely
functioning human face to model and animate

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.030
virtual zombies.

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:11.439
Imagine what artists could do with this, especially
in the presence of super high resolution textures

00:02:11.439 --> 00:02:13.239
and photorealistic rendering.

00:02:13.239 --> 00:02:14.319
Oh, my!

00:02:14.319 --> 00:02:17.959
Another glimpse of the future of computer
graphics and animation.

00:02:17.959 --> 00:02:22.040
Make sure to have a look at the paper for
more applications, for instance, they also

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:26.731
demonstrate the possibility of modifying the
chin and the jawbone.

00:02:26.731 --> 00:02:31.859
They even have some result in simulating the
effect of Bell's palsy, which is the paralysis

00:02:31.859 --> 00:02:34.459
of facial muscles on one side.

00:02:34.459 --> 00:02:38.560
While we're at this high note of illnesses,
if you enjoyed this episode and would like

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:44.079
to support us, you can pick up really cool
perks like early access for all of these episodes

00:02:44.079 --> 00:02:45.110
on Patreon.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:47.180
The link is available in the video description.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.520
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time.

